\cf0 \
	The weap resource, surprisingly, stores info on Nova's weapons. The name of the weap resource is used as the weapon name in the weaponry section of the status display. The first two fields control the duration of different aspects of the weapon:\
\
Reload	The number of frames it takes for one of this weapon to reload. 30 = 1 shot/sec. Smaller numbers yield faster reloads.\
\
Count		The number of frames the weapon's shots travel for before they peter out. 30 = 1 second of life.\
\
\
	The next two fields, MassDmg and EnergyDmg, tell Nova how much damage to do when one of this weapon's shots hits something. Energy damage does damage to shields only, and mass damage does damage to armor only. Further, if a ship has shields, its armor can't be damaged until its shields are knocked down (unless the weapon is a shield-penetrating weapon).\
\
\
The next two fields tell Nova how the weapon should behave in flight:\
\

\f2\i \ul Guidance:	The weapon's guidance mode:
\f1\i0 \ulnone \
	\cf2 -1	Unguided projectile\cf0 \
\
	0	Beam weapon (see below)\
\cf2 \
	1	Homing weapon (see Seeker field below)\cf0 \
\
	2	(unused)\
\cf2 \
	3	Turreted beam\cf0 \
\
	4	Turreted, unguided projectile\
\cf2 \
	5	Freefall bomb (launched at 80% of the ship's current velocity, "weathervanes" into the "wind."\cf0 \
\
	6	Freeflight rocket (launched straight ahead, accelerates to its maximum velocity)\
\cf2 \
	7	Front-quadrant turret, (can fire +/-45\'a1 off the ship's nose) fires straight ahead if no target\cf0 \
\
	8	Rear-quadrant turret (can fire +/-45\'a1 off the ship's tail)\
\cf2 \
	9	Point defense turret (fires automatically at incoming guided weapons and nearby ships)\cf0 \
\
	10	Point defense beam (fires automatically at incoming guided weapons and nearby ships)\
\cf2 \
	99	Carried ship (AmmoType is the ID of the ship class)\cf0 \
\
\
Speed		The weapon's speed (pixels per frame * 100)\
\
\
The next field tells Nova how to handle the ammunition for this weapon, assuming it\'d5s not a fighter bay:\
\

\f2\i \ul AmmoType:			What kind of ammo the weapon uses:
\f1\i0 \ulnone \
	-1			Ignored (unlimited ammo)\
\
\cf2 	0-255			Draws ammo from this type of weapon. (Usually, if your Hector Cannon was of ID 131, you'd set the AmmoType to 3 so it'd use Hector Birdseed Pellets or whatever. However, you could conceivably set it to use ammo from another weapon's supply by setting the AmmoType to something else.)\
\cf0 \
	-999			Ship is destroyed when weapon is fired\
\cf2 \
	-1000 & below	Weapon uses abs(AmmoType+1000)/10 units of fuel per shot.  (example: -1005 = 0.5 units per shot)\cf0 \
                                            \
\
\
The next three fields tell Nova which graphic and sound to use for this weapon, and how to launch it:\
\
\cf2 Graphic		What graphic set to use for this weapon\
	0-63		Use this graphic set (i.e. spin resources 200-263)\cf0 \
\
Inaccuracy		The weapon's inaccuracy as it leaves the ship (ignored for guidance-10 point defense beams)\
	0		Fires straight\
	1 and up	Fires with up to this amount of inaccuracy (in degrees)\
\cf2 \
Sound	Which sound to play when the weapon fires\
	-1	Silent but deadly\
	0-63	Play this sound (snd ID 200-263)\cf0 \
\
\
The next four fields store info on how the weapon behaves when it hits\
something:\
\
Impact		The magnitude of the impact when the shot hits something\
	0		No impact\
	1 and up	This amount of impact, which is inversely proportional to the ship's mass.  (e.g. Missile = 30)\
\
ExplodType		What kind of explosion to show when the weapon hits.\
\cf2 	-1		No explosion\cf0 \
	0-63		This type of explosion\
\cf2 	1000-1063	Explosion type 0-63, plus a random number of type-0 explosions around it\cf0 \
\
ProxRadius		The radius of the weapon's proximity fuse (useful for unguided missiles and bombs)\
	0		Weapon requires direct hit to do damage\
	1 and up	This number of pixels of proximity radius\
\
BlastRadius		The radius of the weapon's blast effect\
	0		No blast effect\
	1 and up	This number of pixels of blast radius\
\
The next field contains some miscellaneous flag info:\
\

\f2\i \ul Flags:	Description:
\f1\i0 \ulnone \
\cf2 0x0001	Spin the weapon's graphic continuously (rate of frame advance is controlled by the BeamWidth field as detailed below)\cf0 \
\
0x0002	Weapon fired by second trigger\
\cf2 \
0x0004	For cycling weapons, always start on the first frame of the animation\cf0 \
\
0x0008	For guided weapons, don't fire at fast ships  (ships with turn rate > 3)\
\
\cf2 0x0010	Weapon's sound is looped rather than played repeatedly\
\cf0 \
0x0020	Weapon passes through shields (use sparingly!)\
\cf2 \
0x0040	Multiple weapons of this type fire simultaneously\cf0 \
\
0x0080	Weapon can't be targeted by point defense systems (works only for homing weapons)\
\cf2 \
0x0100	Weapon's blast doesn't hurt the player\cf0 \
\
0x0200	Weapon generates small smoke\
\cf2 \
0x0400	Weapon generates big smoke\cf0 \
\
0x0800	Weapon\'d5s smoke trail is more persistent\
\cf2 \
0x1000	Turreted weapon has a blind spot to the front\cf0 \
\
0x2000	Turreted weapon has a blind spot to the sides\
\cf2 \
0x4000	Turreted weapon has a blind spot to the rear\cf0 \
\
0x8000	Shot detonates at the end of its lifespan  (useful for flak-type weapons)\
\
\
The next field contains flags that control how a guided weapon (Guidance = 1)\
behaves, as well as some misc flags for other weapon types:\
\
Seeker	0x0001	Passes over asteroids\
		\cf2 0x0002	Decoyed by asteroids\cf0 \
		0x0008	Confused by sensor interference\
		\cf2 0x0010	Turns away if jammed\cf0 \
		0x0020	Can't fire if ship is ionized\
	\cf2 	0x4000	Loses lock if target not directly ahead\cf0 \
		0x8000	May attack parent ship if jammed\
\
\
SmokeSet		Which cicn set to use for this weapon\'d5s smoke trail, if any.  0 = cicn\'d5s 1000-1007, 1 = 1008-1015, etc.  Note that the smoke icons themselves can be any size, but if you use ResEdit\'d5s cicn editor to make each icon only as large as it needs to be, game performance will likely improve.\
\
\cf2 Decay			How fast to decay each shot\'d5s power\
	-1 or 0	Ignored\
	1 and up	Remove one point of mass & energy damage every time this number of frames goes by (1 frame = 1/30 sec.)\cf0 \
\
Particles	Number of particles to generate per frame. Set to zero for no particles\
\
\cf2 PartVel	Particle velocity, from 1 to about 200 or so. Experiment to find useful values.\cf0 \
\
PartLifeMin		Minimum life of a particle from this weapon, in frames.\
\cf2 \
PartLifeMax	Maximum particle life, in frames.\cf0 \
\
PartColor	Particle base color, encoded the same as HTML colors.\
		(RRGGBB)\
\
\
\cf2 BeamLength	The length of the beam created by this weapon, if applicable.\cf0 \
\
BeamWidth		Beam width (actually, radius) in pixels. A BeamWidth of 0 will have no center beam, just corona glow. Note: Lightning beams require a BeamWidth or 1 or greater. Note: For sprite-based weapons that spin continuously, this field controls the time between frames, in 30ths of a second. \
                 \
\cf2 Falloff	The corona falloff. Higher numbers make the corona fall off faster. This value must be between 2 and 16. (since lightning beams have no corona, this is ignored for lightning beams)\cf0 \
                 \
BeamColor		The color of the beam center, encoded as a 24-bit RGB value\
	(00RRGGBB).\
                  \cf2 \
CoronaColor	The color of the beam corona, encoded as a 24-bit RGB value.  Note that since the corona is translucent, it will appear approximately half as bright at its maximum as what you specify in this field. (since lightning beams have no corona, this is ignored for lightning beams)\cf0 \
\
\
SubCount		The number of submunitions to create when the shot reaches the end of its life or detonates because something wanders into its proximity radius. Set to 0 or -1 if unused.\
                  \
\cf2 SubType		The resource ID of the weapon type to create as submunitions.  Anything except beams and fighters is handled.\cf0 \
                  \
SubTheta	The angular error of the submunitions as they are launched, as expressed in degrees error from the carrier weapons' heading.\
\
\cf2 SubLimit	If you have defined a recursively-submunitioning weapon (i.e. one which splits into more copies of itself) this field will allow you to limit the number of recursive splits that happen. This field is ignored if the weapon is not recursively submunitioning.\cf0 \
\
ProxSafety		A time delay for the weapon's proximity fuse, in 30ths of a second. Set to zero for immediate arming on launch.\
\

\f2\i \ul Flags2:	Description:
\f1\i0 \ulnone \
\cf2 0x0001	For cycling weapons, keep the graphic on the first frame until the weapon's ProxSafety count has expired\cf0 \
\
0x0002	For cycling weapons, stop the graphic on the last frame\
\cf2 \
0x0004	Proximity detonator ignores asteroids\cf0 \
\
0x0008	Proximity detonator is triggered by ships other than the target (for guided weapons)\
\cf2 \
0x0010	Submunitions fire toward nearest valid target\cf0 \
\
0x0020	Don't launch submunitions when the shot expires\
\cf2 \
0x0040	Don't show weapon's ammo quantity on the status display\cf0 \
\
0x0080	This weapon can only be fired when there is at least one ship of this ship's KeyCarried type aboard\
\cf2 \
0x0100	AI ships won't use this weapon\cf0 \
\
0x0200	This weapon uses the ship's weapon sprite, if applicable\
\cf2 \
0x0400	Weapon is a planet-type weapon, and can only hit planet-type ships or destroyable stellars\cf0 \
\
0x0800	Don't allow this weapon to be selected or displayed if it is out of \
ammo\
\cf2 \
0x1000	Weapon can disable but not destroy\cf0 \
\
0x2000	For beam weapons, display the beam underneath ships instead of on top of them.\
\cf2 \
0x4000	Weapon can be fired while cloaked.\cf0 \
\
0x8000	Weapon does x10 mass damage to asteroids\
               \
               \
Ionization		The amount of ionization energy to add to the ship that gets hit by this weapon. When a ship is ionized it becomes nearly immobilized until the ionization charge dissipates.\
               \
\cf2 HitParticles		The number of particles to generate when a shot of this type hits a ship or asteroid. Set to zero if unused.\cf0 \
               \
HitPartLife		The average life (in frames) of the hit-particles.\
               \
\cf2 HitPartVel		The speed of the hit-particles; a value of 100 is one pixel per frame.\cf0 \
               \
HitPartColor	The color of the hit-particles, encoded as a 24-bit HTML color\
	(00RRGGBB)\
               \
ExitType	The type of weapon exit point to use for this weapon:\
	\cf2 -1	Ignored - weapon will fire from the center of the ship\
\cf0 	0	GunPosX/Y\
\cf2 	1	TurretPosX/Y\cf0 \
	2	GuidedPosX/Y\
\cf2 	3	BeamPosX/Y\cf0 \
                \
BurstCount		The number of shots this weapon can fire before having to endure a burst reload. Set to 0 or -1 if unused.\
                 \
BurstReload		The reload time that is imposed when the weapon has fired BurstCount shots. Ignored if BurstCount is 0 or -1.            \
 \
             \
JamVuln1-4		The weapon's vulnerability to the four different types of jamming, from 0 to 100%. Ignored if the weapon is not a guided weapon.\
                 \

\f2\i \ul Flags3:	Descriptions:
\f1\i0 \ulnone \
\cf2 0x0001  Weapon will only use ammo at the end of a burst cycle\cf0 \
\
0x0002  Weapon's shots are translucent\
\
\cf2 0x0004  Firing ship can't fire another shot of this type until the previous one expires or hits something.\cf0 \
\
0x0010  Weapon fires from whatever weapon exit point is closest to the target\
\
\cf2 0x0020  Weapon is exclusive - no other weapons on the ship can fire while this weapon is firing or reloading\cf0 \
\
             \
Durability		For guided weapons, this is how many point defense hits a shot from this weapon can take before it is destroyed. Set to 0 for weapons that are immediately destroyed by any PD hits. Ignored for non-guided projectile weapons or beams. (PD damage to guided weapons is calculated as 100% of mass damage plus 50% of energy damage)\
                \
\cf2 GuidedTurn		For guided weapons, this is the turning speed of the weapon.  Higher values yield more maneuverable missiles. Ignored for anything but guided weapons.\cf0 \
                \
MaxAmmo		For ammo-using weapons, this is the maximum amount of ammo per each instance of this weapon. (so, if you have two of these weapons, the max amount of ammo for that weapon type would actually be twice MaxAmmo, and so on)  Set to 0 or -1 if you want the ammo quantity to be constrained by the o\'9ftf resource's Max field instead.\
\
Recoil	The amount of recoil force to apply to the firing ship when this weapon is used:\
\cf2 	0 or -1		No recoil\cf0 \
	Positive values	Thrust the ship backwards\
	\cf2 Negative values	Thrust the ship forwards\
\cf0 	(note that the resulting change to the ship's velocity is inversely proportional to its mass)\
\
\
\cf2 LiDensity	For a beam weapon, entering zero in this field will make it a normal, straight beam weapon. Entering a value greater than zero will make the beam a lightning beam, which has no real effect other than to make it look cool. The number you enter here is the number of zig-zags the beam will make per 100 pixels. Higher numbers yield more convoluted beams. Note also that lightning beams can't have a beam corona, and so will only use the CoreColor and CoreWidth fields above.\cf0 \
\
LiAmplitude	The amplitude of each zig-zag of a lightning beam, in pixels.  Higher numbers yield more jagged-looking beams. Don't overdo this or you will have screen redraw problems. In fact, don't overuse lightning beams period, as they are much more processor-intensive to draw than normal beams!\
\
\cf2 IonizeColor		The color that a ship hit by this weapon will appear after being sufficiently ionized (encoded the same as an HTML color value).  A value of 0 here will be interpreted as a default bluish color. Using fairly bright colors here is probably the best, as low-intensity colors tend to look odd when used as the ionization color.\cf0 \
\
\
Please note that if you've set the weapon to be a beam (Guidance of 0 or 3) the\
following fields have different functions:\
\
\cf2 Count		The number of frames the beam stays onscreen. Note also that if the beam has a positive Decay value entered (see below) the actual time the beam will exist onscreen will be Count + 16 - CoronaFalloff, so adjust the Reload value accordingly to ensure that the universe doesn't get filled up with overlapping beams from a single ships.\cf0 \
\
Impact	Functions normally, with one exception: if the impact is set to a negative value, the beam acts as a tractor beam. Smaller ships will be pulled towards the firing ship with a strength proportional to the Impact value, while a small ship firing a tractor beam at a larger ship (or asteroid) will "latch on" to it and be dragged along. Note that you cannot enter hyperspace if held by a tractor beam from a ship that's larger than you are. Note also that inertialess ships are not affected by tractor beams.\
\
ProxRadius		Ignored\
\
BlastRadius		Ignored\
\
Decay		If Decay is greater than zero, the beam will "shrink" before it disappears from the screen. The actual time the beam spends on screen will be Count + 16 - CoronaFalloff in this case, so adjust your Reload value accordingly (otherwise you'll get too many beams onscreen at once).\
\
\
\
Another Note:  If you don't create an o\'9ftf resource for each weapon type, your\
ship's weapon loadout will be corrupted when you land on a planet.\
(specifically, Nova will mistakenly remove any weapons for which you didn't\
create o\'9ftf resources)  Also, having multiple outfit items that bestow the\
user with the same weapon type can cause weird things to happen.}